# A close of a socket another thread waits on ends the wait, failing
# `HERO_NET_NOT_OPEN`, and nothing reaches the socket after it (feature 2295,
# 2026-11-12): an `accept` waiting on a listener, which on Darwin went on
# waiting and took a connection made after the close (sitting 246's
# ffi-pragmatist); a `recv` waiting on a connection, which read the shutdown
# as the peer's end of stream on Darwin and Linux and was never ended on
# Windows (the box); and a `send` waiting for room in a buffer its peer never
# reads. Each ends now because the layer's calls wait in slices and ask
# between them whether the socket was closed. And the shape beside them
# (lane b34-layer, 2026-11-13): two calls waiting on one socket at once, two
# accepts on one listener and a recv beside a send on one connection, each
# ended by the one close, where the base left both accepts waiting. The
# waiting threads are C's, in the header beside this file: a program's
# Heroes threads carry one `i64`, and a socket is a handle. Each line prints
# 1 where every step held, else the step that did not.
extern "the-platform-layer-ends-a-call-its-socket-closes.h"
    function close_ends_accept() -> i64
    function close_ends_recv() -> i64
    function close_ends_send() -> i64
    function close_ends_two_waits() -> i64

function main()
    print(f"a close ends an accept another thread waits in: {close_ends_accept()}")
    print(f"a close ends a recv another thread waits in: {close_ends_recv()}")
    print(f"a close ends a send another thread waits in for room: {close_ends_send()}")
    print(f"a close ends two calls waiting on one socket at once: {close_ends_two_waits()}")
